{
  "id": "ALAS2023-2026-3441",
  "title": "Amazon Linux 2023 - ALAS2023-2026-3441: Important priority package update for python-pillow",
  "issued": {
    "date": "2026-11-12 09:00:00"
  },
  "updated": {
    "date": "2026-11-12 09:00:00"
  },
  "severity": "Important",
  "description": "Package updates are available for Amazon Linux 2023 that fix the following vulnerabilities:\nCVE-2026-96640:\n\tPillow is a Python imaging library. From 5.1.0 until 13.0.0, PdfParser.linearize_page_tree() in src/PIL/PdfParser.py treats a PDF /Pages graph as a tree without tracking duplicate indirect references. When Image.save(..., format=\"PDF\", append=True) processes an attacker-controlled existing PDF whose /Kids arrays repeatedly reference the same child /Pages node, Pillow expands the shared subtree as distinct pages. A compact malformed PDF can therefore create exponentially many page references and consume disproportionate CPU before the append completes. This issue is fixed in version 13.0.0.\n\nCVE-2026-112902:\n\tA flaw was found in Pillow. When saving an image in the TIFF format, Pillow incorrectly forwards unknown metadata tags to the underlying image processing library. A remote attacker could exploit this flaw by providing a specially crafted image containing malicious metadata to an application that processes and re-saves it. Successful exploitation allows the attacker to read out-of-bounds memory, leading to the disclosure of sensitive process memory or causing the application to crash, resulting in a Denial of Service (DoS).\n\nCVE-2026-112401:\n\tA flaw was found in Pillow. When parsing GIF images with numerous comment blocks, the library repeatedly copies data in an inefficient manner. A remote attacker can exploit this vulnerability by submitting a specially crafted GIF image to an application that processes images using Pillow, causing excessive CPU consumption and leading to a Denial of Service (DoS).\n",
